## DBsubject(Chemistry)
## DBchapter(Gases)
## DBsection(Kinetic Molecular Theory)
## Level(2)
## KEYWORDS('gases', 'pressure', 'number of particles', 'particle behavior')
## Author('Stephanie Bryan')
## Institution(PCC)
## Language(en-US)

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    "PGstandard.pl",
    "PGML.pl",
    "parserPopUp.pl",
    "PGcourse.pl"
);

TEXT(beginproblem());

############################################################
# Images
############################################################

$sampleAImage = image(
    "SampleA.png",
    width    => 300,
    height   => 300,
    tex_size => 500,
    extra_html_tags =>
        'alt="Sample A: eight gas particles moving inside a container."'
);

$comparisonImage = image(
    "LowerN.png",
    width    => 300,
    height   => 300,
    tex_size => 500,
    extra_html_tags =>
        'alt="Four gas particles moving inside a container of the same size as Sample A."'
);

############################################################
# Answers
############################################################

@pressureChoices = (
    "Choose...",
    "increases",
    "decreases",
    "stays the same"
);

$pressureAnswer = PopUp(
    ~~@pressureChoices,
    "decreases"
);

@reasonChoices = (
    "Choose...",
    "Fewer particles collide with the walls, so fewer collisions occur during a given amount of time.",
    "The remaining particles move more slowly and exert less force during each collision.",
    "The particles have less space and therefore collide with the walls more frequently.",
    "The frequency and force of the particle-wall collisions do not change."
);

$reasonAnswer = PopUp(
    ~~@reasonChoices,
    "Fewer particles collide with the walls, so fewer collisions occur during a given amount of time."
);

############################################################
# Problem
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML

##Changing the amount of gas

Gas pressure results from collisions between gas particles and the
walls of their container.

In each particle diagram:

* each circle represents one gas particle;
* each arrow shows the direction and relative speed of a particle; and
* a longer arrow represents a greater particle speed.

### Initial state:

[@ $sampleAImage @]*

### New state:

[@ $comparisonImage @]*

In the new state, there are fewer gas particles. The average particle
speed and the container volume are unchanged.

Compared with the initial state, the pressure in the new state [@ $pressureAnswer->menu() @]*.

Which statement best explains this pressure change?

[@ $reasonAnswer->menu() @]*

END_PGML

ANS($pressureAnswer->cmp());
ANS($reasonAnswer->cmp());

############################################################
# Solution
############################################################

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

The pressure **decreases**.

Because there are fewer gas particles, fewer particles are available
to collide with the container walls. Therefore, fewer particle-wall
collisions occur during a given amount of time.

The average speed has not changed, so the effect of each individual
collision is unchanged.

END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();